Now that we understand the basic contract for how to write a single instruction
we need to start thinking about instructions as they occur sequentially. The 
goal of a computer program is to be able to execute arbitrary code to be able
to solve various problems; not to load a single instruction. 

Another important concept to understand here is that of the program counter
which is another piece of clever circuitry in the CPU.

By default the PC starts at 0 and increments by 1 every clock cycle. It has 
really only 3 modes of behaviour 

PC = PC + 1 # every clock cycle
PC = A #If a C instruction specifies a jump, then PC is set to whatever address
is in register A
PC = 0 # if the program is reset 

The program counter is simply a 16-bit register like the A and D but is wired
up for this particular use case i.e always keeping track of where the program
is supposed to go next. And PC , unlike registers A and D is always active. It 
will always, on every clock cycle, do one of the above 3 things. 

A fun to think about example with this CPU architecture that brings it home is
to think about the vided game consoles that worked with cartridges. When you 
inserted a cartridge, you would press the reset button on the console. This is 
because those video game consoles had a similar architecture, and the game
cartridges were pieces of ROM that got executed as soon as the device booted,
loading the instructions from whatever the cartridge contained, executing
instructions one after the other.

To write an assembler for a CPU, you need to understand the raw instructions, 
not write them, not even be able to read them, but be able to parse them, given
time.

Let's look at the following binary - 

0000000000000101
1110110000010000
0000000000000111
1110001100000110
1110001110010000
0000000000000010
1110101010000111
0000000000000111
1110101010000111


Now bear with me, you don't need to be able to read this, but just once, before
starting to write an assembler, see if you can parse this, sit with a pen and
paper and try. 

Let's look at each instruction - 

0000000000000101

This is an A instruction, denoted by the first bit being 0.

This is simply the number 5 in binary - 101. So the A register gets the number
5 stored into it. The next instruction - 

1110110000010000

This is a C instruction, denoted by the 15th bit being 1. 

Let's keep this here for reference -

1 1 1 a c1 c2 c3 c4 c5 c6 d1 d2 d3 j1 j2 j3 

The next two bits are ignored by convention. 

The next bit denotes whether to load the value from register A or M (0 or 1)
So in this case we are loading from register A.

The next 6 bits are control bits, c1-c6 = 110000

Now remember that the API for this is given to us by the CPU in the form of a
table. The full table will be added in a different file. This combination, 

of a = 0 and c1-c6 = 110000 maps to the instruction - A, meaning the ALU is 
simply instructed to pass throught the value sitting in the A register without
doing any computation on it.

Of the destination bits, only the bit for the D register is a 1, so store the
output in D. Jump bits are all 0 so no op.

So, what do we have so far?

@5 # Store the value 5 in the A register
D=A #Store the value of register A into register D, in this case 5

Alright, moving on...

0000000000000111

Again an A instruction, loading the value 7, which is 111 in binary into 
register A. 

1110001100000110

C instruction, 001100 = computation = output D. 	

The jump bits are 110 which maps to the condition 'jump if ALU output <=0'

Here we introduce a branch, and the jump instruction being true takes us to 
whatever address is in register A(PC = A), which in this instance is 7.
So here if the condition is met we jump to instruction 7 and if the condition
is not fulfilled, then we just continue executing the instructions i.e
 PC = PC + 1.

You can parse the rest of it similarly. It is obvious in an instant how
cumbersome this is. This is where the assembler comes in. To give us a way to 
represent these instructions symbolically to be able to make sense of it in a 
human way. 
